# Unit tests

- **Runner:** JUnit 5 (`org.junit.jupiter`).

## Arrange–Act–Assert (AAA)

Structure every `@Test` (and each logical block in parameterized tests) with section comments:

- `// Arrange` — inputs, fakes, sim state, and anything set in the test body (shared `@BeforeEach` setup counts as arrange; repeat here only when test-specific).
- `// Act` — the single call or short sequence under test (command schedule/run, kinematics step, sim tick loop).
- `// Assert` — `assert*` / `assertTrue` / `assertEquals` only; no new mutations.

Rules of thumb:

- Prefer **one** primary Act per test. Stateful lifecycle tests may use multiple **Act → Assert** pairs (e.g. schedule then cancel).
- One-liner tests: use `// Arrange & Act` then `// Assert`, or split if it stays readable.
- `@ParameterizedTest`: `// Arrange` may note `@CsvSource` / provider; Act and Assert stay explicit.
- Do not omit AAA on “small” tests — consistency beats brevity.
- **Pure logic:** prefer tests that do not need HAL — package-visible helpers, small util/math types, command input mapping, drive kinematics/optimization helpers. No CAN, no full robot graph.
- **HAL / vendor sim:** `@ExtendWith(WpilibSimulationExtension.class)` when tests touch WPILib sim or vendor sim (joysticks, Spark, CTRE). Use isolated CAN IDs; close module/hardware handles in `@AfterEach` when tests construct real devices.
- **Do not** instantiate `Robot` or a full drivetrain subsystem graph in unit tests unless you add a dedicated fixture — extract testable logic instead (see `testsupport/` for shared sim setup).
- After changes, run `./gradlew test` (Java 17 toolchain from `build.gradle`).
